50 pixels in height, 150 pixels in width, and 3 colour channels (RGB).\n- Data type: **uint8** (8-bit unsigned integer).\n- Value range: **0 to 255**.\n- Each image therefore contains 150 × 150 × 3 = **67,500 numbers**. The top-left 3×3 values of the red channel (8, 6, 3, 5, 4) are close to 0, so those pixels are very dark. To a computer, an image is just a tensor of numbers.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"0243155c-2126-4572-9da0-feb0b324e961","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 2: Convolution and Parameters by Hand","metadata":{}},{"id":"58844cf8-a45c-4369-a788-7ff370ab9811","cell_type":"code","source":"# The 5x5 example from the lecture: a vertical edge\nx = torch.tensor([[0, 0, 10, 10, 10]] * 5, dtype=torch.float32)\nk = torch.tensor([[-1, 0, 1]] * 3, dtype=torch.float32)\nout = F.conv2d(x.view(1, 1, 5, 5), k.view(1, 1, 3, 3))\nprint(out.squeeze())            # expect three rows of [30, 30, 0]\n\n# The same kernel on a real photo, converted to grayscale\ng = transforms.functional.to_tensor(img.convert('L')).unsqueeze(0)\nedges = F.conv2d(g, k.view(1, 1, 3, 3), padding=1)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(9, 4))\nax[0].imshow(g.squeeze(), cmap='gray')\nax[0].set_title('Input')\nax[1].imshow(edges.squeeze().abs(), cmap='gray')\nax[1].set_title('Vertical edges')\nfor a in ax:\n    a.axis('off')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:18:54.909548Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:18:54.910074Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:18:55.341899Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:18:54.910045Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:18:55.341038Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"tensor([[30., 30.,  0.],\n        [30., 30.,  0.],\n        [30., 30.,  0.]])\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":3},{"id":"d0a4efba-6949-4f6e-ab77-464d11629a71","cell_type":"code","source":"def count(m):\n    return sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters())\n\nconv = nn.Conv2d(in_channels=3, out_channels=64, kernel_size=3, padding=1)\ndense = nn.Linear(150 * 150 * 3, 512)\nprint(f'Conv 3x3, 3 -> 64   : {count(conv):,} parameters')   # (3*3*3+1)*64\nprint(f'Dense 67,500 -> 512 : {count(dense):,} parameters')\n\nt = torch.randn(1, 3, 224, 224)\nfor stride in [1, 2]:\n    c = nn.Conv2d(3, 64, kernel_size=3, stride=stride, padding=1)\n    print(f'stride {stride}: output {tuple(c(t).shape)}')   # (W-K+2P)/S + 1\nprint('2x2 max pool:', tuple(F.max_pool2d(t, 2).shape))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:19:03.546634Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:19:03.547165Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:19:03.880015Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:19:03.547136Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:19:03.879078Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Conv 3x3, 3 -> 64   : 1,792 parameters\nDense 67,500 -> 512 : 34,560,512 parameters\nstride 1: output (1, 64, 224, 224)\nstride 2: output (1, 64, 112, 112)\n2x2 max pool: (1, 3, 112, 112)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"id":"47242240-6164-4153-849a-e3564eadb01e","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### ✍️ Write it: Part 2\n\n**Convolution layer (3 → 64 channels, 3×3 kernel):**\n- Formula: (K × K × C_in + 1) × C_out\n- (3 × 3 × 3 + 1) × 64 = (27 + 1) × 64 = 28 × 64 = **1,792 parameters**\n\n**Dense layer (67,500 → 512):**\n- Formula: (inputs × outputs) + biases\n- (67,500 × 512) + 512 = 34,560,000 + 512 = **34,560,512 parameters**\n\n**Ratio:**\n- 34,560,512 / 1,792 = **19,286 times more parameters** in the dense layer.\n\n**Output sizes (checked with (W − K + 2P) / S + 1):**\n- Stride 1: (224 − 3 + 2) / 1 + 1 = 224, so the output is (1, 64, 224, 224).\n- Stride 2: (224 − 3 + 2) / 2 + 1 = 111.5 → 111 + 1 = 112, so the output is (1, 64, 112, 112).\n- 2×2 max pool: 224 / 2 = 112, so the output is (1, 3, 112, 112). The channels stay at 3 because pooling works on each channel separately.\n\n**Why weight sharing helps (2 sentences):**\nA convolution slides the same small kernel over every position of the image, so a detector learned once (for example, a vertical edge or a wheel texture) works everywhere. Because of this, the model recognizes the same object whether it appears in the top-left or the bottom-right, and it needs far fewer parameters, which also reduces overfitting.","metadata":{}},{"id":"6419f4a3-0f62-4fa9-a8bf-95b5b280a199","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 3: Transforms and Data Loaders","metadata":{}},{"id":"da9f9460-74b9-4a35-8044-85f93460dd47","cell_type":"code","source":"MEAN, STD = [0.485, 0.456, 0.406], [0.229, 0.224, 0.225]   # ImageNet stats\n\ntrain_tf = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.RandomResizedCrop(224, scale=(0.7, 1.0)),\n    transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(),\n    transforms.ColorJitter(brightness=0.2, contrast=0.2),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize(MEAN, STD)])\neval_tf = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.Resize((224, 224)),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize(MEAN, STD)])\n\n# Two views of the same folder: augmented for training, clean for validation\nfull_aug = datasets.ImageFolder(TRAIN_DIR, transform=train_tf)\nfull_eval = datasets.ImageFolder(TRAIN_DIR, transform=eval_tf)\nidx = np.random.permutation(len(full_aug))\nn_val = int(0.15 * len(idx))\ntrain_ds = Subset(full_aug, idx[n_val:])\nval_ds = Subset(full_eval, idx[:n_val])\ntest_ds = datasets.ImageFolder(TEST_DIR, transform=eval_tf)\n\ntrain_dl = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=64, shuffle=True, num_workers=2)\nval_dl = DataLoader(val_ds, batch_size=128, num_workers=2)\ntest_dl = DataLoader(test_ds, batch_size=128, num_workers=2)\nprint(len(train_ds), 'train |', len(val_ds), 'val |', len(test_ds), 'test')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:19:14.701094Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:19:14.701521Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:19:36.739543Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:19:14.701493Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:19:36.738576Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"11929 train | 2105 val | 3000 test\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"id":"15a6d567-6610-47fc-ba91-73437ff372ee","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### ✍️ Write it: Part 3\n\n**Split sizes:**\n- Train: **11,929** | Val: **2,105** | Test: **3,000**\n- The original training folder has 14,034 images. 15% of it, int(0.15 × 14,034) = **2,105**, was held out for validation, leaving 14,034 − 2,105 = **11,929** for training. The test set (3,000 images) comes from the separate `seg_test` folder and is not used for any choice.\n\n**Augmentation that would be wrong for this dataset:**\n- **Vertical flip.** Scenes have a natural up-down order: sky is above, and ground, sea, or glacier is below. A flipped image (mountain pointing downwards, sea above the sky) never occurs in real photos, so the model would learn from unrealistic data. (Horizontal flip is fine here, because a mirrored forest or street still looks like a real scene.)\n\n**Augmentation that would be wrong for handwritten digits:**\n- **Horizontal flip (or 180° rotation).** A mirrored 2, 3, 5, or 7 is not a valid digit, and a 180° rotation turns a 6 into a 9. This would change the meaning of the image and make the label wrong.\n\n**Key idea:** an augmentation is only safe if it keeps the label true and produces images that could really appear in the data.","metadata":{}},{"id":"62264983-8845-475d-b709-06f8f978fe57","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 4: The Training Loop and Feature Extraction","metadata":{}},{"id":"ac2b901e-f786-461c-926e-dbafb567dabf","cell_type":"code","source":"def run_epoch(model, loader, optimizer=None):\n    \"\"\"One pass over the data. Trains only if an optimizer is given.\"\"\"\n    training = optimizer is not None\n    model.train(training)\n    total_loss, correct, n = 0.0, 0, 0\n    with torch.set_grad_enabled(training):\n        for xb, yb in loader:\n            xb, yb = xb.to(device), yb.to(device)\n            logits = model(xb)                       # forward pass\n            loss = F.cross_entropy(logits, yb)       # softmax + log loss\n            if training:\n                optimizer.zero_grad()\n                loss.backward()                      # backpropagation\n                optimizer.step()                     # gradient step\n            total_loss += loss.item() * len(yb)\n            correct += (logits.argmax(1) == yb).sum().item()\n            n += len(yb)\n    return total_loss / n, correct / n\n\n\ndef fit(model, optimizer, epochs, history):\n    best_acc, best_state = -1.0, None\n    for ep in range(epochs):\n        t0 = time.time()\n        tr_loss, tr_acc = run_epoch(model, train_dl, optimizer)\n        va_loss, va_acc = run_epoch(model, val_dl)\n        history.append((tr_loss, va_loss, tr_acc, va_acc))\n        if va_acc > best_acc:\n            best_acc = va_acc\n            best_state = copy.deepcopy(model.state_dict())\n        print(f'epoch {ep + 1}: train loss {tr_loss:.3f} acc {tr_acc:.3f} | '\n              f'val loss {va_loss:.3f} acc {va_acc:.3f} '\n              f'({time.time() - t0:.0f}s)')\n    model.load_state_dict(best_state)        # keep the best validation epoch\n    return best_acc","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:19:49.210790Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:19:49.211086Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:19:49.220218Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:19:49.211063Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:19:49.219423Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":7},{"id":"7fff74cc-4b92-42fb-9dda-12883c806dd4","cell_type":"code","source":"model = models.resnet18(weights=models.ResNet18_Weights.DEFAULT)\nfor p in model.parameters():\n    p.requires_grad = False                              # freeze the body\nmodel.fc = nn.Linear(model.fc.in_features, len(classes))  # new 6-class head\nmodel = model.to(device)\n\ntrainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\nprint(f'Trainable {trainable:,} of {count(model):,} parameters')\n\nhistory = []\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(model.fc.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nbest = fit(model, opt, epochs=3, history=history)\nprint(f'Best validation accuracy, feature extraction: {best:.3f}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:19:56.196287Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:19:56.196756Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:22:20.959641Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:19:56.196720Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:22:20.958774Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Downloading: \"https://download.pytorch.org/models/resnet18-f37072fd.pth\" to /root/.cache/torch/hub/checkpoints/resnet18-f37072fd.pth\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"100%|██████████| 44.7M/44.7M [00:00<00:00, 195MB/s]\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"Trainable 3,078 of 11,179,590 parameters\nepoch 1: train loss 0.588 acc 0.809 | val loss 0.327 acc 0.894 (62s)\nepoch 2: train loss 0.326 acc 0.890 | val loss 0.290 acc 0.899 (42s)\nepoch 3: train loss 0.299 acc 0.895 | val loss 0.276 acc 0.901 (40s)\nBest validation accuracy, feature extraction: 0.901\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"id":"2d167a1c-780b-4c92-894d-b39a52343520","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### ✍️ Write it: Part 4\n\n**Validation accuracy per epoch (feature extraction):**\n- Epoch 1: **0.894** | Epoch 2: **0.899** | Epoch 3: **0.901**\n- Best validation accuracy: **0.901** (epoch 3). Validation loss also fell steadily: 0.327 → 0.290 → 0.276.\n- Only **3,078 of 11,179,590** parameters were trainable (512 × 6 weights + 6 biases), which is 3,078 / 11,179,590 ≈ **0.03%** of the network.\n\n**Why high accuracy is possible while training only 0.03% of the parameters:**\nResNet-18 was already trained on 1.2 million ImageNet photos, so its frozen layers have learned a hierarchy of features: early layers detect edges and colours, middle layers detect textures and parts, and the last layers detect larger shapes and object-like patterns. These features are general, so they are also useful for scenes such as forest, sea, glacier, mountain, buildings and street (for example, tree textures, water, snow, straight building edges). The network's body therefore already turns each image into a useful 512-number description, and the new `fc` layer only has to learn a simple linear mapping from these 512 features to our 6 classes. That is a small problem, so 3,078 parameters and 3 epochs (about 40 to 60 seconds each) were enough to reach about 90% validation accuracy.","metadata":{}},{"id":"1de3cd5f-f36b-44f1-a2bd-89646abba8f4","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 5: Fine-Tuning and Learning Curves","metadata":{}},{"id":"49141a4f-bff2-48f6-aa43-2421e9998e6f","cell_type":"code","source":"for p in model.layer4.parameters():\n    p.requires_grad = True                      # unfreeze the last block\n\nopt = torch.optim.Adam([\n    {'params': model.layer4.parameters(), 'lr': 1e-4},   # gentle: pretrained\n    {'params': model.fc.parameters(), 'lr': 1e-3}])      # faster: new head\ntrainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\nprint(f'Trainable now: {trainable:,}')\nbest = fit(model, opt, epochs=3, history=history)\nprint(f'Best validation accuracy, fine-tuned: {best:.3f}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:22:37.058050Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:22:37.058588Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:24:39.906134Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:22:37.058557Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:24:39.905271Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Trainable now: 8,396,806\nepoch 1: train loss 0.262 acc 0.906 | val loss 0.227 acc 0.927 (40s)\nepoch 2: train loss 0.170 acc 0.938 | val loss 0.204 acc 0.931 (39s)\nepoch 3: train loss 0.124 acc 0.955 | val loss 0.219 acc 0.929 (43s)\nBest validation accuracy, fine-tuned: 0.931\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"id":"a303d3ca-514a-4a8c-bf6f-45df3c2ed478","cell_type":"code","source":"h = np.array(history)\nep = np.arange(1, len(h) + 1)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 4))\nax[0].plot(ep, h[:, 0], 'o-', label='train')\nax[0].plot(ep, h[:, 1], 'o-', label='validation')\nax[1].plot(ep, h[:, 2], 'o-', label='train')\nax[1].plot(ep, h[:, 3], 'o-', label='validation')\nfor a, title in zip(ax, ['Loss', 'Accuracy']):\n    a.axvline(3.5, color='gray', ls='--')      # where fine-tuning starts\n    a.set_title(title)\n    a.set_xlabel('epoch')\n    a.legend()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:24:46.627476Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:24:46.628269Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:24:46.920969Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:24:46.628236Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:24:46.920019Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":10},{"id":"c6bc9dc3-d935-48c3-9e70-b04e8c45ac4a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### ✍️ Write it: Part 5\n\n**Feature extraction vs fine-tuning:**\n- Feature extraction best: **0.901** | Fine-tuned best: **0.931** | Difference: **+0.030 (3.0 percentage points)**\n- Yes, fine-tuning improved validation accuracy. Trainable parameters went from 3,078 to **8,396,806**, because layer4 was unfrozen (with a learning rate of 1e-4 for layer4 and 1e-3 for the new head).\n- Fine-tuning epochs (overall epochs 4 to 6): val accuracy 0.927 → 0.931 → 0.929. The best epoch (0.931) was epoch 5, and `fit()` restored the weights from that epoch.\n\n**What the curves show:**\n- Loss: training loss falls steadily across all six epochs (0.588 → 0.326 → 0.299 → 0.262 → 0.170 → 0.124). Validation loss falls from 0.327 to 0.204 by epoch 5, then rises slightly to 0.219 at epoch 6. The dashed line at epoch 3.5 marks where fine-tuning starts, and there is a visible drop in validation loss right after it (0.276 → 0.227).\n- Accuracy: validation accuracy rises from 0.894 to 0.931 and then flattens. Training accuracy keeps rising (0.809 → 0.955) and finally passes validation accuracy.\n\n**Overfitting?**\n- **Mild signs, starting at the last epoch.** In epoch 6, training loss keeps falling (0.170 → 0.124) while validation loss rises (0.204 → 0.219) and validation accuracy dips (0.931 → 0.929). The gap between training accuracy (0.955) and validation accuracy (0.929) is also now positive. This is the typical start of overfitting, but it is small. Keeping the best validation epoch (epoch 5) protects us from it, and I would not train more epochs with this setup.\n\n**Why training accuracy can be LOWER than validation accuracy:**\n- In epochs 1 to 4, training accuracy was below validation accuracy (for example, 0.809 vs 0.894 in epoch 1, and 0.906 vs 0.927 in epoch 4). There are two reasons:\n  1. **Augmentation:** training images are randomly cropped, flipped and colour-jittered, so they are harder than the clean, resized validation images. The model has to be correct on distorted versions.\n  2. **Averaging:** training accuracy is the average over the whole epoch while the weights are still improving, whereas validation accuracy is measured once at the end of the epoch with the improved weights.\n- Once the model learned the training data well (epochs 5 and 6), training accuracy rose above validation accuracy.","metadata":{}},{"id":"11012040-e9df-47f2-8f83-6aa6caa09f59","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 6: Test Once, Then Study the Errors","metadata":{}},{"id":"1f43667f-e108-4029-8a27-bac4f38041c5","cell_type":"code","source":"model.eval()\nprobs, preds, labels = [], [], []\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for xb, yb in test_dl:\n        p = F.softmax(model(xb.to(device)), dim=1).cpu()\n        probs.ap