**buildings: 0.906** (precision 0.952). 396 of 437 buildings images were correct, and 35 were predicted as street.\n- The strongest class is forest (recall 0.985, precision 0.994), probably because its green texture is very distinct. The class with the lowest precision is mountain (0.883), because 57 glacier images and a few others were wrongly predicted as mountain.\n\n**Most confused pair (from the confusion matrix):**\n- The largest off-diagonal cell is **glacier (true) predicted as mountain**, with **57** images. The reverse mistake (mountain predicted as glacier) happened 31 times, so the pair glacier ↔ mountain accounts for 57 + 31 = **88 errors**.\n- The second most confused pair is **buildings ↔ street**: 35 buildings predicted as street and 18 street predicted as buildings, **53 errors** in total.\n- Both pairs make sense: glacier and mountain scenes both contain snow, rock and sky, and buildings and street scenes both contain buildings and roads.\n\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"a4dd4549-697b-4da0-b800-4acaf7cbe2f9","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Part 7: Save and Share","metadata":{}},{"id":"77b08d91-23cb-4c6a-a2da-b9bd847b84cc","cell_type":"code","source":"path = '/kaggle/working/resnet18_intel.pt'\ntorch.save(model.state_dict(), path)\nprint(f'Saved {path} ({os.path.getsize(path) / 1e6:.1f} MB)')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T21:32:34.853655Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T21:32:34.854234Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T21:32:34.920245Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-18T21:32:34.854203Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T21:32:34.919282Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Saved /kaggle/working/resnet18_intel.pt (44.8 MB)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":14},{"id":"79de68ae-cd5b-4d6e-9102-f5984c9278fa","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Check Your Understanding\n\n**1. Why does a network without nonlinear activations collapse to a single linear layer?**\n\n> A linear layer computes W·x + b. Stacking two of them gives W2(W1·x + b1) + b2 = (W2·W1)·x + (W2·b1 + b2), which is again of the form W·x + b with a new weight matrix W = W2·W1 and a new bias. By induction, any number of linear layers is equivalent to one linear layer, so depth adds no extra power. A nonlinear activation (such as ReLU) between the layers breaks this and lets the network learn non-linear patterns.\n\n**2. For logits [3, 1, 0], what probability does softmax give the first class?**\n\n> softmax(z_i) = e^(z_i) / Σ e^(z_j)\n> - e^3 = 20.086, e^1 = 2.718, e^0 = 1\n> - Sum = 20.086 + 2.718 + 1 = 23.804\n> - P(class 1) = 20.086 / 23.804 = **0.844 (about 84.4%)**\n> (For comparison: P(class 2) = 0.114 and P(class 3) = 0.042; the three add up to 1.)\n\n**3. How many parameters does a 3 x 3 convolution from 64 to 128 channels have?**\n\n> Parameters = (K × K × C_in + 1) × C_out\n> = (3 × 3 × 64 + 1) × 128 = (576 + 1) × 128 = 577 × 128 = **73,856 parameters**\n> (73,728 weights + 128 biases)\n\n**4. An input of 56 x 56 goes through a 3 x 3 convolution with padding 1 and stride 2. What is the output size?**\n\n> Output = (W − K + 2P) / S + 1 = (56 − 3 + 2) / 2 + 1 = 55 / 2 + 1 = 27.5 + 1\n> The division is rounded down (27), so the output size is 27 + 1 = **28 × 28**.\n\n**5. Why does a residual (skip) connection help gradients reach the early layers?**\n\n> A residual block computes y = F(x) + x. When we differentiate, dy/dx = dF/dx + 1, so the gradient always has an identity path (the \"+1\") that goes straight back to earlier layers without being multiplied by many small weights or activation derivatives. In a plain deep network the gradient is a long product of small numbers and can vanish before it reaches the early layers. The skip connection avoids this, so very deep networks (like ResNet) can still be trained.\n\n**6. When would you train a CNN from scratch instead of using transfer learning?**\n\n> When the target data is very different from ImageNet photos (for example, medical scans, spectrograms, or multispectral satellite images with a different number of channels), so the pretrained features do not transfer well. Also when we have a very large labeled dataset that can support training from scratch, or when we need a much smaller or custom architecture (for example, for a tiny embedded device) than the pretrained models offer.","metadata":{}},{"id":"0180f624-4a95-4970-a682-45c9781ee3b4","cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}